< description:`Starts a workflow recipe and returns once the run is started: a workflowPath launch returns immediately; a workflowPrompt launch first runs the workflow-creator inside the call (a design pass that can take several minutes), then launches. Provide exactly one of workflowPath or workflowPrompt (automatic creation), plus input values (workflowPath form only).\n\nWORKFLOWPROMPT FORM (automatic creation):\n- Pass \`workflowPrompt\` with a task brief instead of naming a recipe: the workflow-creator agent designs and saves a definition, and the same call launches it \u2014 one call replaces the workflow-creator dispatch + run_workflow pair. The call blocks while the creator works (progress shows on the tool card) and returns when the generated run has started. The brief must be self-contained (the creator sees nothing else from this conversation): the goal, absolute paths, decisions already made, constraints, worktree instructions, and verification steps. Mutually exclusive with workflowPath and inputs \u2014 the creator determines the generated workflow's inputs.\n\nWORKFLOWPATH FORMS:\n- Existing recipes available to this session (workspace > user > cloud > bundled):\n${$}\n${$}- "generated://<id>" \u2014 relaunches a definition saved by an earlier \`workflowPrompt\` launch that failed to start: the failure result carries the ref and its inputs, so pass them back here instead of paying for another creator dispatch. Single-use: the stored definition is consumed when the run starts, so relaunching a run that did start requires a fresh save.\n- "agent://<name>" \u2014 runs one registered agent from the AVAILABLE AGENTS list above as a one-step workflow. Requires a non-empty 'prompt' input, used verbatim as the step's prompt; an optional 'report_path' input is recorded as the step's report artifact. Model and effort inherit from the parent session. Top-level sessions only \u2014 nested launches from workflow steps are refused.\n- An absolute filesystem path to a .workflow.json, .workflow.yaml, or .workflow.yml file in an admitted recipe root: an active workspace's .kiro/workflows/, the user-level ~/.kiro/workflows/, ~/.kiro/cloud-cache/resolved/config/user/workflows/, or ~/.kiro/cloud-cache/resolved/config/organization/workflows/. Pass catalog workflowPath values verbatim. When a recipe is not in the catalog, locate it with your file-inspection tools, which resolve ~ against the agent's configured home; do not shell out with ~ or $HOME, which resolve to a different home in a cloud sandbox.\nDo NOT pass the filesystem path of a bundled recipe (e.g. a path inside the agent package); use its bundled://<name> URI instead.\nThere is no inline definition form: to run a definition you already have as JSON, write it to <workspace>/.kiro/workflows/<name>.workflow.json and launch it by absolute path.\n\n${$===void 0?"":`${$}\n\n`}RUN LABEL:\n- Without a runLabel, every run shows the recipe's static name; whenever you launch a reusable recipe pass a descriptive <recipe>-<topic> label (e.g. runLabel: "investigate-dock-title" for bundled://investigate on a dock-title bug) so run lists and notifications stay distinguishable.\n\nBEHAVIOR:\n- Creates the workflow, invokes it, and returns with the workflowId and status as soon as the run is started. With workflowPath that is immediate; with workflowPrompt the call first waits for the workflow-creator's design pass, and its result also carries the creator's SUMMARY of what was designed.\n- The run itself is not awaited: the tool never blocks on workflow steps.\n- Progress notifications arrive as messages from the agents running workflow steps.\n- Nested workflow invocations are not supported: a workflow step cannot start its own workflow.\n- Use inspect_workflow to check status at any time.\n`,agentsIndex:$("agents",$,$($))}
